#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179490 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc9469a-e2ca-55ba-b05a-b0be37384f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases"}] :question/id #uuid "ccfb8833-f270-5855-8c49-fb3b6b6662d5" :question/math-academy-id "e-18776" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation $5^{x+2}=9^{x-2}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $\\left(\\ln\\right)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x+2} & ={9}^{x-2} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x+2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({9}^{x-2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(x+2)\\ln(5) = (x-2)\\ln(9).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =(x-2)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) & =-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)) & =2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)) \\\\ x & =\\frac{2(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9))}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179505 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e37891e-d24f-53cd-ba74-d887fa99aa3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions of Exponential Equations"}] :question/id #uuid "453d4ec3-98f5-5db1-a046-a6b15c13049d" :question/math-academy-id "e-4097" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of $\\log_2,$ what value of $x$ satisfies the equation $2^{x+1}=3^x?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take $\\log_2$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{x+1} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ {log}_{2}⁡({2}^{x+1}) & ={log}_{2}⁡({3}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\log_b \\left( b^c \\right) = c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+1 = \\log_2 \\left(3^{x}\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+1 = x \\log_2 \\left(3\\right)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x{log}_{2}⁡(3) \\\\ x{log}_{2}⁡(3)-x & =1 \\\\ x({log}_{2}⁡(3)-1) & =1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{1}{{log}_{2}⁡(3)-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179506 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/id #uuid "fbb90aa1-5adf-5c6d-8499-68eea953cc42" :question/math-academy-id "e-4098" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation $e^{x+1}=2^x\\cdot 5?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $\\left(\\ln\\right)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+1} & ={2}^{x}\\cdot 5 \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x}\\cdot 5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\ln \\left( e^a \\right) = a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+1 =\\ln \\left(2^{x}\\cdot 5\\right)\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power and product laws of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x})+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x+1 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-1 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-1}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542161 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc9469a-e2ca-55ba-b05a-b0be37384f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5fffbee3-1efd-5c7e-b929-db8595281c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541615} {:answer/id #uuid "779296de-c678-56a0-b745-33f46a92ec78" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541669} {:answer/id #uuid "8ae906cb-f33c-5000-b7e1-a999a3299585" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541703} {:answer/id #uuid "bbb5e694-f356-55aa-9b89-ab2bbf3dccc8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541812} {:answer/id #uuid "c8bc1a13-7b5e-55c0-a06e-96d0bba6e79b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5fffbee3-1efd-5c7e-b929-db8595281c83" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541615} :answer-field/id #uuid "f75f0550-f223-5620-afe3-60a4b1e7b6bb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542099}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "708af368-563d-5975-a829-b0da728a3e85" :question/math-academy-id "q-100470" :question/problem "Find the solution of ${5}^{x+3}={2}^{5-4x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x+3} & ={2}^{5-4x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{x+3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{5-4x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =(5-4x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+3)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =(5-4x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x & =\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186237793 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc9469a-e2ca-55ba-b05a-b0be37384f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "3bbf73f4-86d1-5bfd-9e9d-5a3cfdc63e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236191} {:answer/id #uuid "3cac3a9a-26b3-5ebf-89d6-f1f0bfe75ca1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236198} {:answer/id #uuid "50640e0e-0e74-5967-8122-cf3f2c127102" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236332} {:answer/id #uuid "6764564c-a351-5594-9622-c052823ed9f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236469} {:answer/id #uuid "d1e7fc27-1a16-5599-b3af-e337af933f67" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237144}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6764564c-a351-5594-9622-c052823ed9f1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186236469} :answer-field/id #uuid "56ba9cac-4726-57ff-bcea-b0fc9a870e93" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186237554}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "dd5c6b81-63d3-5a9b-b188-f8be38d27f58" :question/math-academy-id "q-100471" :question/problem "Find the solution of ${3}^{x+5}={7}^{6-2x}.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{3}^{x+5} & ={7}^{6-2x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({3}^{x+5}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({7}^{6-2x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(6-2x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =(6-2x)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) \\\\ x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7) & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ x(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)) & =6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3) \\\\ x & =\\frac{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)-5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(7)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186180141 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6a9c2945-081c-5afd-a5c3-bf5b1a6127d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\ln(2)+1}{3-\\ln(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541637}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6a9c2945-081c-5afd-a5c3-bf5b1a6127d6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{\\ln(2)+1}{3-\\ln(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541637} :answer-field/id #uuid "30df98ac-26e2-575f-9740-2cdd94af54be" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186178420}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "3a4d2c64-ecf2-540e-86cd-1d47fbb20b4c" :question/math-academy-id "q-233243" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${e}^{3x+1}={2}^{x-1}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3x+1} & ={2}^{x-1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3x+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x-1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{x-1}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+1 & =(x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x+1 & =(x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ 3x+1 & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ 3x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-1 \\\\ x(3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)) & =-(\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1) \\\\ x & =-\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+1}{3-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542160 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e37891e-d24f-53cd-ba74-d887fa99aa3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions of Exponential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "16446a4c-7a1c-59f3-bc40-4c1c6c59865f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.194" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541467}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "16446a4c-7a1c-59f3-bc40-4c1c6c59865f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-2.194" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541467} :answer-field/id #uuid "2690682d-4da1-5e1c-906c-371ce3d099aa" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541981}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7088fa93-cf81-5263-ba93-771873a7af12" :question/math-academy-id "q-242900" :question/problem "Given that ${5}^{x+4}={2}^{2-x},$ what is the value of $x?$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places.\n\n$x≈$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we take ${log}_{5}$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{x+4} & ={2}^{2-x} \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡({5}^{x+4}) & ={log}_{5}⁡({2}^{2-x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+4={log}_{5}⁡({2}^{2-x})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx+4=(2-x){log}_{5}⁡(2)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+4 & =(2-x){log}_{5}⁡(2) \\\\ x+4 & =2{log}_{5}⁡(2)-x{log}_{5}⁡(2) \\\\ x+x{log}_{5}⁡(2) & =2{log}_{5}⁡(2)-4 \\\\ x(1+{log}_{5}⁡(2)) & =2{log}_{5}⁡(2)-4 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2{log}_{5}⁡(2)-4}{1+{log}_{5}⁡(2)}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the change of base formula, we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{5}⁡(2)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}≈0.430\\,677.\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $x$ as\n\n$$\nx≈\\frac{2\\cdot 0.430\\,677-4}{1+0.430\\,677}≈-2.194,\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542214 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "692b80fe-4651-538d-b5d1-e61580f22f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\ln(3)}{2-\\ln(8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541633}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "692b80fe-4651-538d-b5d1-e61580f22f9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\ln(3)}{2-\\ln(8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541633} :answer-field/id #uuid "c51625d8-026e-5ce6-a283-a81c61b0638d" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542057}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "e0454be3-0e05-5ea2-b188-0b8e56e969a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-242901" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${e}^{2x-1}=3\\cdot {8}^{x}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{2x-1} & =3\\cdot {8}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{2x-1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot {8}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n2x-1=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3\\cdot {8}^{x}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power and product laws of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({8}^{x}) \\\\ 2x-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x-1 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ 2x & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)+1 \\\\ 2x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+1 \\\\ x(2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(3)+1}{2-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290535 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "36d18ca3-aa8a-5c2b-8ba9-858b796a780c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\operatorname{ln}(6)}{3-2\\operatorname{ln}(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186288399} {:answer/id #uuid "6f1a8c1d-34c3-5f3f-802b-b86ca9ece3f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\ln(6)}{3-2\\ln(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541645}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6f1a8c1d-34c3-5f3f-802b-b86ca9ece3f2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1+\\ln(6)}{3-2\\ln(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541645} :answer-field/id #uuid "1991aa2c-1472-5830-92f9-81b17a0bd32f" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289792}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1285c9cd-7326-5f21-8cc4-238b71cf12c1" :question/math-academy-id "q-242903" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${e}^{3x-1}={6}^{2x+1}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{3x-1} & ={6}^{2x+1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{3x-1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{2x+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\n3x-1=\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{2x+1}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n3x-1=(2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3x-1 & =(2x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ 3x-1 & =2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ 3x-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ x(3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)) & =1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ x & =\\frac{1+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}{3-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542165 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1d8620a1-45c2-5921-8e00-1ee9c4b575d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3+\\ln(5)}{1-2\\ln(8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541481}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d8620a1-45c2-5921-8e00-1ee9c4b575d5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{3+\\ln(5)}{1-2\\ln(8)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541481} :answer-field/id #uuid "c7b3bf75-0b93-51c5-bf35-20bc18d4efe5" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542060}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "8c789237-199d-5f09-befa-8f93e41fb414" :question/math-academy-id "q-242905" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${e}^{x-3}=5\\cdot {8}^{2x}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x-3} & =5\\cdot {8}^{2x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x-3}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5\\cdot {8}^{2x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives\n\n$$\nx-3=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5\\cdot {8}^{2x}).\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power and product laws of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({8}^{2x}) \\\\ x-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-3 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) \\\\ x-2x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8) & =3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x(1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)) & =3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x & =\\frac{3+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{1-2\\operatorname{ln}⁡(8)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542209 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc9469a-e2ca-55ba-b05a-b0be37384f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4e287ef6-a5a0-55d7-96bb-18ad9b59ae5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln(4)+6\\ln(9)}{3\\ln(4)-\\ln(9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541582}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4e287ef6-a5a0-55d7-96bb-18ad9b59ae5a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\ln(4)+6\\ln(9)}{3\\ln(4)-\\ln(9)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541582} :answer-field/id #uuid "01305e7b-645f-5e06-ae80-c90e114cac25" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541964}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ce76cbe9-5155-5439-bee7-2d4f7a68d153" :question/math-academy-id "q-242913" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${4}^{3x-1}={9}^{x+6}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{4}^{3x-1} & ={9}^{x+6} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({4}^{3x-1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({9}^{x+6})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(3x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)=(x+6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(3x-1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) & =(x+6)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ 3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ 3x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x(3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9) \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)+6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}{3\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(9)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542119 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc9469a-e2ca-55ba-b05a-b0be37384f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "87ff023a-a134-5ecf-9d84-a60f06cfe9da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\ln(6)-\\ln(2)}{6\\ln(2)-\\ln(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541697}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "87ff023a-a134-5ecf-9d84-a60f06cfe9da" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{7\\ln(6)-\\ln(2)}{6\\ln(2)-\\ln(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541697} :answer-field/id #uuid "1100e579-fc88-5143-8fa1-3bda713a1cde" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541973}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "144d3b46-ddcf-55fb-8478-125713df09d1" :question/math-academy-id "q-242915" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${2}^{6x+1}={6}^{x+7}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{2}^{6x+1} & ={6}^{x+7} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({2}^{6x+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{x+7})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(6x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)=(x+7)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(6x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =(x+7)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ 6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) & =x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)+7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ 6x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x(6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)) & =7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2) \\\\ x & =\\frac{7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)}{6\\operatorname{ln}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186290541 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179789 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "2dc9469a-e2ca-55ba-b05a-b0be37384f9e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm: Advanced Cases"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "de8c4e1d-1932-5465-80c5-a8d6e9673b95" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}(6)-\\operatorname{ln}(5)}{4\\operatorname{ln}(5)-7\\operatorname{ln}(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186289512} {:answer/id #uuid "eeb781b7-1b70-57f0-a8c2-0887631a7392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\ln(6)-\\ln(5)}{4\\ln(5)-7\\ln(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541928}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "eeb781b7-1b70-57f0-a8c2-0887631a7392" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{5\\ln(6)-\\ln(5)}{4\\ln(5)-7\\ln(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541928} :answer-field/id #uuid "8a7b683d-9fcf-5ed1-8675-d32910b910e0" :answer-field/key "field-1" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045492 :db/ident :answer-field.type/blank} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290031}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "c423a638-983b-5ebe-b413-4d69284f1a8b" :question/math-academy-id "q-242919" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, determine the value of $x$ that satisfies the equation ${5}^{4x+1}={6}^{7x+5}.$\n\n$x=$\n{{field-1}}" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{4x+1} & ={6}^{7x+5} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({5}^{4x+1}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{7x+5})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify both sides of the equation. This gives\n\n$$\n(4x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)=(7x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6).\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(4x+1)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =(7x+5)\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ 4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) & =7x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)+5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ 4x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x(4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)) & =5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5) \\\\ x & =\\frac{5\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{4\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-7\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186292682 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e37891e-d24f-53cd-ba74-d887fa99aa3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions of Exponential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "18f85746-b79b-5580-b5d4-13f1d8e44462" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.293" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290737} {:answer/id #uuid "33cde5e3-9a3b-53ea-b943-70c8a99494f5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-5.891" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290840} {:answer/id #uuid "35d82a02-03b6-51c5-8449-78b2bc84ce9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.213" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290852} {:answer/id #uuid "4a79d869-63a0-57b2-96e1-c9779852a986" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.712" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290948} {:answer/id #uuid "fc971f9e-6d7d-5373-b123-f07b18462734" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.544" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291706}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "35d82a02-03b6-51c5-8449-78b2bc84ce9e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "6.213" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186290852} :answer-field/id #uuid "191c3242-0209-5594-87e0-47ca785c4d4f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186291830}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7d6a09c3-0907-53a4-ad37-e4629c10f3e2" :question/math-academy-id "q-26828" :question/problem "Given that ${5}^{y}={4}^{y+1},$ what is the value of $y?$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, we take ${log}_{5}$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{5}^{y}={4}^{y+1} \\\\ {log}_{5}⁡({5}^{y}) & ={log}_{5}⁡({4}^{y+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & ={log}_{5}⁡({4}^{y+1})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(y+1){log}_{5}⁡4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $y,$ as follows: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y & =(y+1){log}_{5}⁡4 \\\\ y & =y{log}_{5}⁡4+{log}_{5}⁡4 \\\\ y-y{log}_{5}⁡4 & ={log}_{5}⁡4 \\\\ y(1-{log}_{5}⁡4) & ={log}_{5}⁡4 \\\\ y & =\\frac{{log}_{5}⁡4}{1-{log}_{5}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the change of base formula, we have\n\n$$\n{log}_{5}⁡(4)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(4)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}≈0.861\\,353.\n$$\n\nFinally, then, we can approximate $y$ as\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}y≈\\frac{0.861\\,353}{1-0.861\\,353}≈6.213,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nto $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542116 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e37891e-d24f-53cd-ba74-d887fa99aa3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions of Exponential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "41bac9b3-13f7-5b8b-a321-f1d007e4bfa8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "7.891" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541561} {:answer/id #uuid "d16681c8-41f9-5f36-a7de-90f0e375ea52" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-10.934" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541867} {:answer/id #uuid "dfb8c2e8-dfe1-54d9-9a97-0a6f51e8c3e8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "8.901" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541898} {:answer/id #uuid "e3ce0c7a-2d06-5133-af07-3fbd01d22a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9.413" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541905} {:answer/id #uuid "fbf377cf-e020-545e-b783-2989924ac608" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-6.678" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541950}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e3ce0c7a-2d06-5133-af07-3fbd01d22a1d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-9.413" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541905} :answer-field/id #uuid "792b62eb-9bf9-54a8-88d7-aa878a9e5b83" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542024}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "1016352e-7a56-5245-a46f-5f32ded11ee4" :question/math-academy-id "q-26830" :question/problem "Given that $\\frac{{3}^{x}}{5}=3\\cdot {4}^{x},$ what is the value of $x?$ Round your answer to $3$ decimal places." :question/worked-solution "First, notice that, by multiplying by $5$ on both sides,  the given equation is equivalent to\n\n$$\n{3}^{x}=15\\cdot {4}^{x}\n$$\n\nNow, we take ${log}_{3}$ on both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n{log}_{3}⁡({3}^{x})={log}_{3}⁡(15\\cdot {4}^{x})\n$$\n\nThen, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{3}⁡(15\\cdot {4}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNext, we use  the product law and the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{3}⁡(15\\cdot {4}^{x}) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(15)+{log}_{3}⁡({4}^{x}) \\\\  & ={log}_{3}⁡(15)+x{log}_{3}⁡4\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x & ={log}_{3}⁡(15)+x{log}_{3}⁡4 \\\\ x-x{log}_{3}⁡4 & ={log}_{3}⁡(15) \\\\ x(1-{log}_{3}⁡4) & ={log}_{3}⁡(15) \\\\ x & =\\frac{{log}_{3}⁡(15)}{1-{log}_{3}⁡4}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nUsing the change of base formula, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{log}_{3}⁡(15)=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡15}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}≈2.464\\,974, \\\\ {log}_{3}⁡4=\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡4}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡3}≈1.261\\,860.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can approximate $x$ as\n\n$$\nx≈\\frac{2.464\\,974}{1-1.261\\,860}≈-9.413,\n$$\n\nrounded to $3$ decimal places."}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542149 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e37891e-d24f-53cd-ba74-d887fa99aa3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions of Exponential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "07c51994-a16e-53c5-9f4b-087d4584a0b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541438} {:answer/id #uuid "193d4f96-76f2-5425-9669-b57d4ff37861" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541472} {:answer/id #uuid "23c339b2-9834-5db4-b684-69e13092c852" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)+\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541497} {:answer/id #uuid "30102e12-e4ae-556a-874d-a561ad884f6b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-\\frac{3}{\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541518} {:answer/id #uuid "88716cb2-3b00-516a-bad7-a9836ec1eaee" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541698}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07c51994-a16e-53c5-9f4b-087d4584a0b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2}{1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541438} :answer-field/id #uuid "dfc1aca4-c021-5871-bb55-6eb2d01facdd" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542074}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "5c4b6334-f991-59a6-8939-03bf51753adb" :question/math-academy-id "q-66873" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of common logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation ${10}^{x-2}={3}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the common logarithm $(log)$ of both sides of the equation: \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{x-2} & ={3}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{x-2}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx-2=\\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{x})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\nx-2=x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x-2 & =x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & =2 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)) & =2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2}{1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542197 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179787 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "7e37891e-d24f-53cd-ba74-d887fa99aa3b" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Exact Solutions of Exponential Equations"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "39245218-c124-5e3b-9885-ad36c021185d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}{1+\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541546} {:answer/id #uuid "393def19-6af1-5280-a953-dc5078e18f2a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1+\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541547} {:answer/id #uuid "90a6f8e6-6d98-51f3-9401-29c3de7e41d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)-1}{1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541719} {:answer/id #uuid "d51d8d66-8e5b-5398-a14f-9cb65b564558" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡(2)-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541877} {:answer/id #uuid "db1a2cbf-86a0-53b0-8ba7-b2badd721bad" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)-1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541890}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90a6f8e6-6d98-51f3-9401-29c3de7e41d1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)-1}{1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541719} :answer-field/id #uuid "87b34590-cca4-5e71-8290-c7d0e45d01f3" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542032}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "b722f56c-7d4f-5883-9de4-4bfad6f8a324" :question/math-academy-id "q-66883" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of common logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation ${10}^{x+1}={3}^{x+2}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the common logarithm $(log)$ of both sides of the equation:   \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{10}^{x+1} & ={3}^{x+2} \\\\ \\operatorname{log}⁡({10}^{x+1}) & =\\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{x+2})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that ${log}_{b}⁡({b}^{c})=c$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+1=\\operatorname{log}⁡({3}^{x+2})\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power law of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =(x+2)\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) \\\\ x+1 & =x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+1 & =x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)+2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{log}⁡(3) & =2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)-1 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)) & =2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)-1 \\\\ x & =\\frac{2\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)-1}{1-\\operatorname{log}⁡(3)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542150 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186179788 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5eb24b54-bdb8-5269-bd38-d346a52bbc6e" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Solutions of Exponential Equations Using the Natural Logarithm"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "278e1e4d-ad3d-58a9-9f28-2ed71db3867a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-2}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541503} {:answer/id #uuid "7fe44be4-f50f-5059-bb83-6a786ecd0393" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541682} {:answer/id #uuid "c83a1383-eca7-566e-b701-873a010c76c0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541845} {:answer/id #uuid "f014ea56-e8db-53d8-8bff-f2a5694d2bc7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-2}{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541930} {:answer/id #uuid "fb1a71b0-bc39-5683-bfcc-2bf35e9e7552" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)}{2+\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541948}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "278e1e4d-ad3d-58a9-9f28-2ed71db3867a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-2}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541503} :answer-field/id #uuid "e0622c41-0b51-5a7f-a242-55040c5a180b" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542077}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "5e9937d3-e10e-5251-81d5-87fad6e6c69a" :question/math-academy-id "q-79363" :question/problem "Expressed in terms of natural logarithms, what value of $x$ satisfies the equation ${e}^{x+2}=5\\cdot {6}^{x}?$" :question/worked-solution "First, we take the natural logarithm $(ln)$ of both sides of the equation:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{x+2} & =5\\cdot {6}^{x} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{x+2}) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5\\cdot {6}^{x})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we use the fact that $\\operatorname{ln}⁡({e}^{a})=a$ to simplify the left-hand side. This gives:\n\n$$\nx+2=\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5\\cdot {6}^{x})\\,\n$$\n\nNext, we use the power and product laws of logarithms to simplify the right-hand side:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+\\operatorname{ln}⁡({6}^{x}) \\\\ x+2 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we solve for $x$, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x+2 & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)+x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) \\\\ x-x\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-2 \\\\ x(1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)) & =\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-2 \\\\ x & =\\frac{\\operatorname{ln}⁡(5)-2}{1-\\operatorname{ln}⁡(6)}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
